concept document_schema {
  name = "Document Schema"
  summary = "Marking a document root with @document, and how schemas merge per namespace."
  related = []
  audience = :both
  body {
    h2 "Composing document schemas"
    p <<DOC
      Several `@document` schemas can govern the same namespace, and they **merge**: a top-level
      field or block is legal if **any** of them declares it. This lets you import a library that
      ships its own `@document` and still add your own top-level tags.
    DOC
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        import <wdoc.wcl>            // brings in wdoc's library @document

        @document
        type ProjectDoc {           // your own root schema — merges with wdoc's
          @children("project_meta") metas: list<ProjectMeta>
        }

        @block("project_meta")
        type ProjectMeta { @inline(0) id: identifier  owner: utf8 }

        project_meta info { owner = "Wil" }   // your tag, alongside wdoc pages
        page index { text { span "Hello" {} } }
      DOC
    }
    p <<DOC
      Imported (library) `@document` schemas merge silently. Only a **second root-authored**
      `@document` in the same namespace is an error — you get one root schema, which composes with
      whatever the imports provide.
    DOC
    callout "Reflection" {
      class = ["info"]
      body = "decorator_names(T) and decorator_arg(T, name, slot) read decorators back at evaluation time — used by libraries (like wdoc) that dispatch on a block's declared kind."
    }
  }
}
